{"id": "en_blackout_city_p2", "total": 64.83, "segments": [{"text": "Part two. Silent carbon monoxide fills the building because someone lit a camp stove downstairs.", "start": 0.0, "end": 4.3}, {"text": "Mark smells nothing, but his battery detector chirps and alerts him to danger right now.", "start": 4.92, "end": 8.02}, {"text": "He acts fast and chooses to walk to shelter on a lower floor near fresh air.", "start": 8.44, "end": 11.32}, {"text": "Mark covers his mouth, grabs his supplies, and decides to enter shelter in the stairwell.", "start": 11.92, "end": 15.08}, {"text": "One. Mark opens exterior doors immediately to ventilate the deadly gas out of the building.", "start": 15.79, "end": 19.54}, {"text": "Never run a generator, grill, or camp stove indoors because carbon monoxide kills silently fast.", "start": 19.92, "end": 24.04}, {"text": "Two. Mark uses bright flashlights only because candles cause terrible house fires during major blackouts.", "start": 24.25, "end": 29.24}, {"text": "Battery lights protect his dark shelter safely without creating any open fire hazards in darkness.", "start": 29.24, "end": 33.46}, {"text": "Three. Mark keeps the fridge closed tight so critical food stays safe for 4 hours.", "start": 33.434, "end": 37.72}, {"text": "A full freezer left completely closed keeps essential food safe for about 48 hours straight.", "start": 38.14, "end": 41.306}, {"text": "Mark stores fresh water early by filling all containers and the bathtub while taps still work.", "start": 42.5, "end": 45.84}, {"text": "Filling containers and the bathtub early secures clean life water during a multi day blackout.", "start": 46.26, "end": 49.56}, {"text": "Fresh air clears the dark hallway as people safely evacuate the building into the night.", "start": 50.28, "end": 53.36}, {"text": "Mark stays calm inside his ventilated room while total darkness covers the silent city grid.", "start": 53.84, "end": 57.36}, {"text": "Never run fuel stoves indoors and always